#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171672 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7da9cbe0-e57b-5289-be33-c21af3f68ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Primary Roots of Unity"}] :question/id #uuid "7e092359-a42e-57c0-9bd7-02976a3a8b1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14557" :question/problem "Find the principal $6$th root of unity." :question/worked-solution "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. This means that\n\n$$\nz^{6} = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\mid 1 \\mid = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\arg(1) = 0\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\nz^{6} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}}⟹z = (e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}})^{1/6} = e^{kπ\\textrm{i}/3}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1$, and is therefore equal to $e^{\\pi \\textrm{i}/3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171673 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/id #uuid "869cc486-15ff-5d48-b535-4c0717e96ca5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14558" :question/problem "Sketch a diagram showing all the $4$th roots of unity." :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $4$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n- one of the roots is $1$, corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n- the roots form a square (regular $4$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/28513be60912977c95ecf4ec773d3792.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171674 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "53d895db-472c-5375-bd35-f1c6e0c6305b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14559" :question/problem "Find the root of unity highlighted in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/98e9ea0ab66a999ba39a7d107f156d9f.png)" :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0)$.\n\nSince there are $7$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $7$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\mid 1 \\mid = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\arg(1) = 0\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\nz^{7} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}}⟹z = (e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}})^{1/7} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}/7}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/047d35f0458fccba0e9e329b3fa9f8c2.png)\n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5$. Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nz_{5} = e^{2(5)π\\textrm{i}/7} = e^{10π\\textrm{i}/7}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa9e87f9-dbe3-55c6-8f71-d5ac64bf74b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3400/Source/Images/q-162254-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169082} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c51fe3-1985-5b3f-892a-31f6551e2d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/340